First Author: Lisboa
Title: Enhanced survival prediction using explainable artificial intelligence in heart transplantation
Source number: 32  ￼

Very short summary: This study evaluated an explainable artificial intelligence approach for predicting 1-year mortality after heart transplantation and benchmarked it against deep learning and conventional models. Five key findings were: (1) the main blinded external validation cohort included 4,750 UNOS transplants from 2017–2018, with an additional Scandinavian external cohort of 2,293 transplants; (2) the final PRN-Lasso model retained 9 univariate effects plus 1 two-way interaction; (3) in the UNOS 2017–2018 external cohort, PRN-Lasso achieved AUROC 0.628, comparable to the deep IHTSA model at 0.635; (4) the PRN model showed good calibration in the blinded external cohort with Hosmer–Lemeshow chi-square 15.01, p = 0.135; and (5) key effects included recipient age, donor age, creatinine, ischemic time, ventilator at transplant, infection within 2 weeks, recipient female sex, transplant year, diagnosis, and an interaction between recipient age and ischemic cardiomyopathy. Figures 1–4 and Tables 1–3 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

